#!/usr/bin/env bash
# init: install and configure the plugin. (REQUIRED)
#
# Run by dot-init for every enabled plugin whose `installed` hook fails, and by
# `dot-plugin run init NAME`. Afterwards `installed` runs again; if it still fails, the
# plugin is reported as failed.
#
# Contract
#   Exit 0      success.
#   Exit != 0   failure. The output (stdout and stderr) is shown, the plugin is reported as
#               failed, and dot-init carries on with the next plugin.
#   Be rerunnable: a failed or interrupted run will be tried again from the start, so use
#   `mkdir -p`, check before cloning, and prefer idempotent commands.
#   Be non-interactive: standard input is closed, so a prompt gets end-of-file.
#   Output is captured and only shown on failure, behind a progress spinner, so print
#   whatever helps you debug a failure.
#   There is no time limit, so a long install is fine.
#   Needs root (system packages)? Add an empty file named `needs-sudo` to the plugin folder.
#   The runner then asks for the sudo password once, in the terminal, before the hook starts;
#   inside the hook run privileged commands with `sudo -n`, which fails at once instead of
#   waiting for a password nobody can see. (A password prompt in a hook would hang.)
#
# Environment
#   DOT_PLUGIN      this plugin's name
#   DOT_PLUGIN_DIR absolute path of this plugin's folder (also the working directory), handy
#                   for shipping data files next to the hook
#   DOT_HOOK        "init"
#   DOT_OS          linux | macos | other
#   DOT_ROOT        the dotfiles worktree, normally $HOME
#   DOT_COMMON      path to common.sh; `source "$DOT_COMMON"` loads shtuff: info, warn,
#                   error, install (system packages; auto-detects apt, dnf, ... but not
#                   Homebrew, so use `brew install` on macOS), copy, move, delete, ...
#                   Note that shtuff's `install` shadows the system `install` command and
#                   `error` does not exit.
#
# Arguments: none.
#
# Good to know
#   - Don't write into tracked paths of your dotfiles repo (they would show up as changes).
#     Keep downloaded software under ~/.local/share/NAME, which the whitelist ignores.
#   - Don't print or store secrets; the output is shown on failure.
#   - Pin versions (a tag or release) so every machine ends up with the same thing.
#   - macOS ships bash 3.2 and BSD tools. A hook runs under the interpreter on its `#!` line,
#     so avoid bash 4 features (or ship a separate `init.macos`) if it must run there.
